199:The first chain-walking helper is \texttt{findRootThink(int startLayer)}. The ensemble-coupling design read the immediate caller's think directly as \texttt{baseClientsThink[callerTaskLayer]}, a single index one hop up. This is only valid in depth 2 fixtures, when the immediate caller is the root REF, but fails when an Immediate intermediate sits between the REF and the callee.  This instead causes \texttt{baseClientsThink[callerTaskLayer]} to return \texttt{0}, so the callee sees no upstream load at all. \texttt{findRootThink} replaces this single index with a walk up the chain, via \texttt{queueNameToLayer.get("T:" + callerName)}, until a REF task caller layer is reached (\texttt{R:} prefix). We also implement a \texttt{[task $\rightarrow$ caller-service]} injection so that for caller task layers, the caller's service demand is overwritten with \texttt{R\_task\_total}, via \texttt{setService(callerServerClass, Exp.fitMean(safeD))}, so the callee response reaches the caller's MVA solve. Finally, in our results reporting, we set \texttt{taskResidT} zero for non-REF tasks whose summed activity \texttt{getHostDemandMean()} is effectively zero (Immediate tasks). This ensures the propagated callee response is not double-reported as the intermediate's own service. 
