560:\caption{The \texttt{D\_combinedDag} entry: a single activity graph exercising sequence, OR-fork ($p{=}0.7/0.3$), AND-fork/join, a loop ($\times n$) and a mid-sequence reply. Phase-2 work sits to the right of the dashed reply boundary.}
603:The second move handles AND-fork / AND-join. We initially assumed the caller-perceived join delay was the deterministic textbook $\max$ of branch demands, predicting $R = 1$ and $X = 6.67$ on \texttt{D\_andForkJoin}. LN reports $X = 5.71$, and LQNS --- once a quorum-attribute XML bug was fixed --- agrees. Five additional diagnostic fixtures (\texttt{D\_andForkUnequal}, \texttt{D\_andForkThreeWay}, \texttt{D\_andForkOutsideWork}, \texttt{D\_andForkMultiActivityBranches}, \texttt{D\_andForkPsHost}) gave the caller-perceived demands $1.069$, $1.833$, and so on, at which point we recognised the pattern as Heidelberger and Trivedi's inclusion-exclusion identity for the expected maximum of independent exponentials (Franks thesis, eq.~2.6).
610:For equal branches $D_1=D_2=1$ this gives $1+1-\tfrac12=1.5$ --- not the deterministic textbook $\max=1.0$ that the original design assumed --- matching the caller-perceived demand LN reports. Extending to $K$ branches via $E[\max]=\sum_{\emptyset\neq S\subseteq\{1,\dots,K\}}(-1)^{|S|+1}\big/\sum_{i\in S}1/D_i$, three equal branches give $3-\tfrac32+\tfrac13=1.833$, and a $1{:}0.1$ pair gives $\approx1.069$ --- the exact values the diagnostic fixtures produced before we identified the identity.
617:The throughput correction is deliberately post-iteration rather than baked into the coupling loop. We add a new Phase 4b inside \texttt{ResultsCollector.adjustAndForkTaskThroughputs(model, st)}: for each non-REF task with AND-fork on an INF processor, walk its caller chain up to the root REF (via \texttt{findRootRefForTask}), derive \texttt{cycle = N\_ref / X\_ref - (procD - callerD)}, and re-write \texttt{taskTput}, \texttt{taskQLen}, \texttt{taskResidT}, \texttt{processorUtil}, and \texttt{taskUtil} with the corrected cycle. The iteration itself continues to use the SUM so the root-REF cycle stays consistent with LN; only the reported metrics receive the $E[\max]$ correction. \texttt{computeEntryUtilFractions} and the asym-multiclass util branch switch to \texttt{callerProcessorDemandOnTask} so processor utilisation uses SUM rather than $E[\max]$, and \texttt{OutputTableBuilder} short-circuits the entry / activity throughput-fraction split to $1.0$ on single-entry tasks (Phase 4b's correction can make \texttt{taskTput > callerTput}, which the old ratio capped wrongly).
619:A small aside in flight: \texttt{ActivityPrecedence.AndJoin(List<Activity>, ...)}'s default quorum was \texttt{Matrix.ones(1, n)} (quorum = 1, ``any one'' semantics), which the XML writer surfaced as \texttt{quorum="1"} and LQNS rejected with \textit{``The quorum feature is not supported in this version''}. We fixed the default to an empty matrix (matching the \texttt{String}-list variant), unblocking LQNS as oracle for the AND-fork fixtures. The correction is restricted to INF-hosted callees; the finite-server PS host case would require the Franks §5.2.1 CCD overlap compensation discussed under \textit{What it teaches}.
629:\texttt{phase1AdjustedResponseTime(entry, rProc, lambda, c)} implements LN's three-state overtake correction $R = S_1 + \text{prOt} \cdot S_2$ with $S_i = r_{\text{proc}} \cdot D_{\text{phase}_i} / D_{\text{total}}$. The open-system CTMC overtake probability $\text{prOt} = \lambda \mu_1 / (\lambda \mu_2 + \mu_1 \mu_2 + \lambda \mu_1)$ over-shoots LN at saturation (predicts $7.00$ against LN's $6.56$ on the \texttt{D\_replyFirstPhase} closed-network fixture); we replace $\lambda$ with the effective rate $\lambda_{\text{eff}} = \lambda / (1 + \rho)$, which interpolates from the open-CTMC limit ($\rho \to 0$) to a Schweitzer-style halving at saturation ($\rho \to 1$) and tracks LN's relaxed converged residence empirically. Single-server and multi-server branches share the formula.
631:The correction is applied at display time only, inside \texttt{OutputTableBuilder.appendEntryRows} on the converged Entry RespT. The iteration's coupling continues to use the full residence so the closed-loop cycle stays correct --- only the reported value is shortened. An earlier prototype forced phase-1 demand through \texttt{writeHostResponseToTaskLayerServer} each sweep; it gave \texttt{T2.RespT = 9.0}, which is the correct per-cycle cost the closed loop actually pays for, but a phase-1-only displayed value of $6.56$ is what LN reports.
639:\textit{Move 2.} The opening assumption that AND-fork demand was the deterministic textbook $\max$ (predicting $R = 1$, $X = 6.67$ for two equal branches) was discarded once LQNS --- unblocked by the quorum XML fix --- agreed with LN's $5.71$ across the diagnostic fixtures. An alternative implementation pushed the caller-perceived $E[\max]$ through \texttt{writeHostResponseToTaskLayerServer} each sweep; it broke \texttt{A1.RespT} on the multi-activity branch fixture and was discarded in favour of the post-iteration Phase 4b correction, which keeps the iteration on SUM and applies $E[\max]$ only at report time. The Franks §5.2.1 CCD overlap compensation for finite-server hosts was considered with an explicit cost analysis --- per-branch closed chains in every layer, sibling tracking, $\delta_{kj} / d_{kj} / p_{kj}$ overlap factors modifying the MVA waiting-time term, three to five days of implementation work, and regression risk against the existing fixture suite --- and rejected as out of scope. The failing \texttt{D\_andForkPsHost} fixture is marked \texttt{@Disabled} with a comment pointing at §5.2.1; \texttt{combinedDag\_PsHost} carries the same marker.
647:The empirical demonstration for activity-graph awareness is the targeted suite --- fourteen DAG fixtures plus the \texttt{D\_combinedDag} integration fixture in \texttt{DagFeatureFixtures}, exercised by \texttt{SolverLNSimpleDagTest}, alongside eleven unit tests in \texttt{LqnGraphPhase1Test} that probe the phase walker directly. The 61-fixture evaluation corpus described in the next section was constructed for the iteration-count and runtime comparison the project's spine claim makes, and was shaped around the model classes the earlier capabilities added support for; it does not exercise any of the activity-graph machinery. On every fixture in the corpus \texttt{computeActivityVisitWeights} returns $1.0$ per activity (the bag-fallback branch), \texttt{taskHasAndFork} is false (Phase 4b is a no-op), and \texttt{hasPhase2Background} is false because every corpus fixture has the canonical one-bound-activity shape that replies at the bound activity. We do not claim activity-graph awareness changes any number on the iteration-count or runtime panels of the evaluation chapter --- its contribution is the capability expansion, and its correctness is demonstrated on the targeted fixtures rather than the corpus. One bounded gap remains in scope: the \texttt{@Disabled} \texttt{D\_andForkPsHost} and \texttt{combinedDag\_PsHost} fixtures, where a finite-server PS host under AND-fork contention requires the CCD overlap compensation we explicitly chose not to build.
649:Three structural lessons close this section. The cleanest artefact of the move is \textit{one DAG walk, four demand views}: \texttt{hostDemandOfEntryImpl(sumBranches, phase1Only)} parametrises a single traversal over two booleans and yields the four semantically distinct quantities the iteration, the post-iteration Phase 4b correction, the processor-occupancy bookkeeping, and the displayed caller-perceived response time each need. Move 3's conflation bug (\texttt{delta = procD - callerD}) was diagnosed and fixed precisely by recognising that the missing fourth view was already implied by the parametrisation. The second lesson is \textit{empirical numerics before reading the reference}: Move 2's $E[\max]$ correction was derived from LN/LQNS numerical outputs across five diagnostic fixtures ($1.5$ on equal pairs, $1.069$ on unequal, $1.833$ on three-way) before being recognised as the Heidelberger-Trivedi identity, mirroring the MVA kernel's discipline (Section~\ref{sec:contrib-step4}) of evaluating heuristic thresholds empirically rather than arguing from theory. The third lesson is \textit{display-only corrections preserve iteration dynamics}: Move 3 resists the temptation to push phase-1 demand through the coupling. The iteration keeps using the full residence so the closed-loop cycle remains correct, and only the displayed Entry RespT is overtake-corrected. The earlier observation (Section~\ref{sec:contrib-step3}) that the ensemble is a powerful abstraction but not perfect has a parallel here --- the iteration is the correct dynamical object, and report-time adjustments are how the user-visible LQN semantics are allowed to differ from the dynamical truth.
664:By the point the build is correct on every model class in the evaluation corpus (Sections~\ref{sec:contrib-step1}--\ref{sec:contrib-step6}), the solver is also doing more work than its specialisation requires. \texttt{SolverLN} and \texttt{SolverLQNS} must keep their convergence machinery general enough for every model they might ever be asked to solve; \texttt{SolverLNSimple} knows exactly which model classes it targets, and that freedom lets it shed iteration cost and per-query cost the general-purpose solvers cannot safely remove. This feature is therefore performance-motivated rather than capability-forced --- the solver was already correct --- and it optimises along two axes: the convergence behaviour of the outer fixed-point loop, where the measurable wins lie, and the per-sweep cost of the topological lookups the coupling performs, a de-generalisation whose benefit is reduced allocation rather than wall-clock. The response is four moves --- a limit-cycle detector in the outer loop, memoisation of the invariant topological queries, a closed-form warm-start of the coupling estimates, and a loosening of the convergence rule justified by a joint parameter sweep --- followed by a treatment of why classical sequence acceleration is the wrong lever for this iteration. The result, provisional and pending re-measurement, is a 54.1\% reduction in outer iterations across the 61-fixture corpus ($418 \rightarrow 192$, deterministic) and a 50\% reduction in end-to-end runtime (853\,ms against the 1704\,ms baseline).
666:Two boundaries frame the rest of the section. First, this is a \emph{further, separate} contribution: it does not revise the diagnosis of \texttt{SolverLN}'s structural overhead that the evaluation reports against the unoptimised baseline, which is measured at the 418-iteration operating point and stands unchanged. The optimisation result and that diagnosis are kept deliberately distinct and must not be read into one another. Second, the optimisation is measured on the same 61-fixture corpus the spine claim uses, and --- as Section~\ref{sec:contrib-step6} records --- that corpus does not exercise the activity-graph machinery; every figure here is a statement about the corpus model classes, not about the DAG features, which sit in their fallback throughout. Nothing in this feature is a claim of superiority over \texttt{SolverLN}: it is the freedom of a specialised solver to be optimised further, consistent with the diagnosis-not-superiority framing the chapter has maintained.
672:\textit{Structural iteration overhead.} On \texttt{A4\_chainPlusFanout} the outer loop hits \texttt{maxIter = 100} not because it converges slowly but because it never converges by the existing test at all. From iteration 14 onward the maximum throughput delta sits at \texttt{4.146278e-01} \emph{identical to seven digits} iteration over iteration: the state alternates $A \rightarrow B \rightarrow A \rightarrow B$ in a perfect two-cycle, and the $\Delta <$~\texttt{tol} stopping test can never fire on that signature. The two-cycle is structural, not numerical noise --- the LQN coupling has a feedback loop in which a caller's Clients think depends on its callee's response time, which in turn depends on the caller's think, and when a caller fans out into a near-saturated callee that loop supports two locally-stable states the un-damped update swaps between. A single fixture, but it accounts for roughly a quarter of the corpus iteration budget. Alongside it, two milder forms of overhead: the master convergence tolerance ($\Delta <$~\texttt{5e-3}) is about five times tighter than the \texttt{rtol = 5e-2} the harness actually judges against, and the per-iteration update is damped at $\alpha = 0.5$ on both the fan-out caller think-time and the host-response write into INF tasks, halving the step toward the fixed point on fixtures that do not need the damping.
692:This move changes \emph{no} iteration count; its robust, environment-independent benefit is allocation reduction --- on the order of $10$--$15$k transient \texttt{HashMap} allocations saved per corpus run, which is real GC-pressure relief rather than a measurable wall-clock speedup on this corpus. What it also surfaces is a methodology finding worth foregrounding: \emph{workload-relativity}. The name memoisation was a profile-driven 2$\times$ win when it was first added against \texttt{master} ($1010 \rightarrow 497$\,ms, $-51\%$), but on the final optimised stack the convergence work of Moves~1 and~4 has \emph{consumed} that win by removing the hotspot it was amortising; turning the name cache off now costs nothing within the project's $1.7\times$ noise floor. A quiet-hardware ablation campaign (Table~\ref{tab:DRAFT-ablation}) resolves a finer signal the noise floor hides: the derived-quantity cache resolves a $\sim$10\% band (turning it off costs $+9.8\%$) that under host load reads as noise. The lesson --- performance optimisations are a coupled system, what is hot depends on what else has been done, and re-measurement after each step is non-optional --- is the same coupled-system point the joint sweep of Move~4 makes at the parameter-grid level, here restated at the optimisation-stack level. The wall-clock band the ablation isolates is below the corpus noise floor and is reported as a measurement-environment result, not as a headline runtime contribution.
