713	Iteration~1 starts from the ensemble initialiser's cold defaults, so on fan-out and multi-coupling topologies the first outer pass is spent discovering coupling estimates from scratch. The warm-start writes a closed-form initial Clients-think value into each \texttt{T:} layer \emph{before} the loop, using exactly the unsaturated branch of \texttt{computeCallerClientsThink} that iteration~1 would otherwise compute: \texttt{z = max(refChainThink, directThink) + siblingBlocking}, clamped and written via \texttt{setService}. This is a coupling warm-start, not cache warming --- it sets an algorithmic state variable of the outer iteration (the Z estimate), not the data cache of Move~2, which would populate at iteration~1 regardless. Nor is it a more-work-per-iteration trade: it adds microseconds of formula evaluation ahead of the loop, and iteration~1's MVA solves with warm Z are the same operations as with cold Z, on better inputs. The saving concentrates on the slow fan-out fixtures --- \texttt{B\_scaleAxis\_tasks40\_fanout} and \texttt{B\_scaleAxis\_tasks60\_fanout} both fall from 5 to 3 outer iterations, an 18\% per-fixture runtime reduction that is clean above noise on those fixtures --- while chain fixtures see no change, because the warm-start formula's unsaturated branch is essentially the chain-demand propagation that iteration~1 of any chain solve produces anyway. Across the corpus it saves 10 outer iterations deterministically; the aggregate wall-clock effect is sub-noise because the real savings live in a handful of fixtures. The mechanism is a textbook closed-form initial estimate for a fixed-point iteration rather than a novel contribution, it is toggle-able via \texttt{lnsimple.warmStart=false} for ablation, and its only standing cost is that the formula must stay in sync with \texttt{computeCallerClientsThink}'s unsaturated branch.
714	
715	\subsubsection*{Move 4: loosening the convergence rule}
716	
717	\texttt{SolverLNSimple} is positioned as an \emph{efficient} solver, not a high-accuracy one: \texttt{SolverLN} and \texttt{SolverLQNS} are the accuracy ground truth, and the harness checks \texttt{SolverLNSimple} against the LN reference at \texttt{atol = 1e-3}, \texttt{rtol = 5e-2}. Against that backdrop the master convergence rule is overspecified in two coupled ways --- the stopping tolerance gate and the per-iteration damping --- and both are loosened together because neither pays off in isolation. Master's damping was not arbitrary: its own comments record that for callers invoking more than one distinct callee the new Z is under-relaxed because ``MVA's response slope in Z is steep near saturation $\ldots$ so plain fixed-point iteration alternates between saturated and unsaturated states'', and that INF tasks on multi-server PS processors damp the D write ``since \texttt{R\_proc} fluctuates between bare-demand and queueing-tail values across sweeps''. That phenomenon is real, and the sweep below confirms it has a genuine stability cliff. The point is only that \texttt{master} sat at $\alpha = 0.5$, a full half-step inside the safe region, while the cliff is near $\alpha = 1.0$; and that the case the damping was best designed to fix --- the \texttt{A4\_chainPlusFanout} alternation --- it never actually fixed (the fixture stayed at 100 iterations for every $\alpha \in [0.1, 1.0]$ at the master tolerance), because that case is the structural two-cycle Move~1 now handles directly.
718	
719	The decisive evidence is that a one-knob-at-a-time sweep \emph{rejects} this entire move: at the master \texttt{tol = 5e-3} the $\alpha$ sweep finds zero sensitivity (398 iterations at every $\alpha$), because the tolerance, not the step size, is the bottleneck --- every configuration must run enough iterations to reach the same precision regardless of step. Only a joint $(\alpha, \text{tol})$ sweep (Table~\ref{tab:DRAFT-alpha-tol}) reveals that the two levers unlock each other: a larger step only pays off once the tolerance is loose enough that fewer iterations clear the gate, and the looser tolerance only stays inside the accuracy envelope once the larger step puts each iterate closer to the fixed point. The $\alpha = 1.0$ row is the only zero-regression row at every tolerance; the \texttt{tol = 1.5e-2} column is regression-free \emph{only} at $\alpha = 1.0$ (at $\alpha = 0.5$ it fails on \texttt{A3\_multiserver\_c2\_loadLow}, the smallest multi-server fixture, which has no headroom); and true over-relaxation at $\alpha = 1.2$ breaks catastrophically (slack of order $10^{15}$ on \texttt{A4\_chainPlusFanout.E3.RespT}, 429 on \texttt{B\_scaleAxis\_tasks5\_fanout}). The chosen cell $(\alpha = 1.0,\ \text{tol} = 1.5e\text{-}2)$ is one notch inside the cliff in every direction.
