810	\section{Instrumentation: the measurement apparatus}
811	\label{sec:contrib-instrumentation}
812	
813	Throughout this section, and in our evaluation chapter, we rely on two key pieces of instrumentation to make the spine claim measurable. The first is a correctness check. For each model in our test corpus, we run \texttt{SolverLNSimple}, \texttt{SolverLN}, and \texttt{SolverLQNS}. The test is marked as passing our solver matches either solver within 5\% tolerance on all metrics. We check both mature solver outputs as they sometimes differ in results. The second is the performance counters. Iteration counts for each solver are retrieved (\texttt{iters[0]}, \texttt{SolverLN.maxitererr.size()} and \texttt{SolverLQNS.result.iter} respectively), along with a clock execution time, allowing us to compare performance across solvers. 
814	
815	The iteration counters are not strictly comparable across solvers. \texttt{SolverLN}'s counter includes the hard-reset verification pass, while the simple solver's does not, therefore the asymmetry biases the comparison slightly toward the simple solver. Execution time is not strictly comparable either, as \texttt{SolverLNSimple} and \texttt{SolverLN} are running in the same JVM while \texttt{SolverLQNS} calls an external process, which is written in C\#. Despite this, they both work as practical performance comparison metrics.
