819	\section{Closing remarks}
820	\label{sec:contrib-closing}
821	
822	The chapter has presented \texttt{SolverLNSimple} as a set of capabilities: a minimal LQN solver and the features that extend it, a performance optimisation of the resulting correct solver (Section~\ref{sec:DRAFT-perf-optimisation}), a short instrumentation section that makes their claims measurable, and a discussion against related work and the evaluation results. Each capability responded to a specific model failure that a design without it could not handle, and the machinery it adds is what its specific failure demanded. The \texttt{LqnGraph} abstraction emerged from the graph-derived coupling work (Section~\ref{sec:contrib-step2}), accumulated through multi-class support and fan-out handling, and became the class the codebase organises around. The design choice of the project was to always pick the solution that requires the least reworking/extra machinery, to prevent the code from becoming overly complex.
823	
824	The chapter has not measured what falls out. Evaluation reports the iteration counts and accuracy data on closed-LQN fixtures spanning single-class chains, multi-class shared-callee topologies, deep IS topologies at large populations, fan-out callers with sibling callees and the multi-class-multi-entry interactions. We compare against \texttt{SolverLN} \cite{casale2024} and LQNS V5 \cite{franks2009,franks2022manual}. The structural claim the chapter has made, that the simple solver omits adaptive under-relaxation, moving-average convergence with hard-reset verification, and the unconditional \texttt{ptaskcallers} random walk, and that those omissions are not failures but designs, is what makes the iteration-count comparison meaningful when Evaluation reports it. The contribution is the construction itself, with its characterisation that the bottom-up build hits fewer iterations than both comparators, and the per-machinery decomposition in Evaluation showing that most of the iteration-count difference with \texttt{SolverLN} is structural rather than algorithmic, but not universally unnecessary.
