\subsubsection*{Classical sequence acceleration is the wrong lever}

Aitken's $\Delta^2$ extrapolation, Anderson acceleration, Nesterov momentum, and Broyden quasi-Newton iteration were each implemented and swept, and each was rejected; the implementations remain in the source gated by system properties (defaulting off) so the ablations can be reproduced. Aitken extrapolates three iterates of a linearly-converging sequence to its fixed point; Anderson generalises this to a least-squares fit over a window of recent residuals; Nesterov extrapolates ahead by a momentum term before each step; Broyden maintains a rolling inverse-Jacobian estimate and takes a Newton-like step. All four assume one or more of: that the iteration is locally smooth (Nesterov, Broyden); that the contraction is linear-rate at a moderately slow ratio (Aitken and Anderson buy the gap between a linear ratio $\rho$ and a super-linear $\rho^2$, which is negligible when $\rho \approx 0.25$ as the B-chain trace shows); and that there are enough iterations to fill the acceleration window (a depth-$m$ Anderson needs $\ge m+1$ iterations before it can fire; Broyden needs $\ge n$ to estimate an $n$-dimensional inverse Jacobian).

The LQN decomposition iteration has none of these properties. Because the per-layer MVA is \emph{exact}, the outer contraction is governed by LQN topology, not by customer population: adding customers makes the inner solve harder but leaves the outer iteration count small (typically 3--10) at every model size, so the acceleration windows can never fill --- even \texttt{tasks40\_chain} and \texttt{tasks60\_chain} converge in three outer iterations, which refutes the natural expectation that the big fixtures would reward Broyden (they are big in $N$, not in iteration count, and the inverse-Jacobian estimate never receives a useful update). The contraction is already strong, leaving no super-linear margin to recover. And the genuine pathologies are non-smooth structural two-cycles from caller--callee blocking feedback, not slow linear tails: the correct response to a two-cycle is to detect and exit, not to extrapolate the alternating values. Worse, the smoothing methods are \emph{actively} harmful on this structure --- Nesterov's momentum amplifies the very oscillation it is meant to smooth (24--65\% iteration inflation, because boosting a sign-alternating step pushes harder in the wrong direction each iteration and also corrupts the limit-cycle detector's $\Delta$ signal), and Broyden inflates iterations by 60--150\% with eight correctness regressions, its Jacobian estimate jumping between the two locally-stable states of the cycle. The right levers, empirically, are the structure-specific ones of Moves~1, 3 and~4 --- cycle detection, over-relaxation, and tolerance/warm-start tuning --- not classical convex-optimisation primitives. The lesson is that ``sophisticated'' does not mean ``applicable'': the method has to match the structure of the problem, and a well-augmented Picard iteration beats sophisticated acceleration here.

\subsubsection*{Sweep order}

The default \emph{bounce} sweep (a $2N-1$ up-and-down pass per outer iteration) was re-examined against four alternatives --- forward, reverse, double-forward, and the \emph{true-elevator} cross-iteration alternation that \texttt{SolverLN} and \texttt{SolverLQNS} use --- in a three-phase study (Table~\ref{tab:DRAFT-sweep}). True-elevator, the mature-solver pattern, is strictly dominated (317 vs.\ 192 iterations: its cross-iteration alternation wastes half its passes going the wrong way). Reverse marginally beats bounce on the corpus (191 iterations, 13\% faster) and was promoted for closer inspection --- but it is a trap. On synthetic stress fixtures targeting topologies the A/B/C corpus does not sample, reverse fails silently: on \texttt{DS2}, three reference tasks fanning in to a shared callee chain leading to a saturated leaf, reverse reports a queue length of 14.23 against the LN ground truth of 20.05 (29\% wrong) while bounce holds to 0.2\%. The mechanism is that fan-in saturation produces a non-triangular dependency graph in which Gauss--Seidel ordering becomes load-bearing rather than asymptotically irrelevant: reverse solves the shared callee first with all caller throughputs stale, the stale sum drifts further than any single term, and the iteration settles into a self-consistent-but-wrong fixed point with utilisation pinned at 1.0. This is the same non-uniqueness of the iteration map under saturation that produces Move~1's two-cycle --- here a one-state version (settling monotonically into the wrong basin) rather than an oscillating one. Bounce's two-direction pass forces per-iteration consistency and locks onto the correct basin; its $2N-1$ cost is the price of robustness across topology classes the corpus does not exercise, so it stays the default, with \texttt{lnsimple.sweep=reverse} retained as an opt-in for workloads known to be chain-dominated. Two methodology points fall out: a benchmark corpus is not a topology class (reverse looked like a free win until the stress fixtures exposed the failure mode), and session-1's reported ``sweep-order invariance'' was an artefact of the master damping --- under the now-default $\alpha = 1.0$, sweep order moves the iteration count by 2--4$\times$, a second instance of damping masking sensitivity to set beside the $(\alpha, \text{tol})$ interaction of Move~4.

