\subsection{What optimising the specialised solver provides}

The optimised solver converges on the 61-fixture corpus in 192 outer iterations against the unoptimised 418 (a 54.1\% reduction, deterministic and identical across runs) and in 853\,ms end-to-end against 1704\,ms (a 50\% reduction); both figures are provisional and pending re-measurement. Roughly half the runtime saving follows from the fewer iterations the convergence moves produce (limit-cycle detection, over-relaxation, looser tolerance, and warm-start), and the name memoisation was the other half historically against \texttt{master} --- now workload-relative, its win consumed by the convergence work, with the de-generalisation specialisations contributing robust allocation relief rather than a measurable corpus speedup. These numbers are a separate contribution from, and do not revise, the diagnosis of \texttt{SolverLN}'s structural overhead the evaluation reports at the unoptimised baseline; the two are measured at different operating points and are reported distinctly. They are also a statement about the corpus model classes only: the optimisation does not exercise the activity-graph machinery, which remains in its fallback throughout, and it is not a superiority claim --- it is what a solver freed from general-purpose constraints can do to itself.

Three structural lessons close the feature. The first is \emph{workload-relativity}: an optimisation stack is a coupled system, each layer's value depends on what else has been done, and a profile-driven win can be silently consumed by a later change, so re-measurement after each step is non-optional and a na\"ive ``every cache helped'' narrative is wrong. The second is that \emph{joint sweeps catch what one-knob sweeps miss when knobs interact}: a marginal sweep rejected the entire convergence-rule move (zero $\alpha$-sensitivity at the master tolerance), and only the joint $(\alpha, \text{tol})$ grid revealed the cliff at the sweet spot --- the same pattern that exposed the sweep-order sensitivity the master damping had hidden. The third is the \emph{specialisation thesis at its honest level}: the simple solver is free to specialise precisely because it is not a general-purpose meta-solver, and that freedom is real in two registers --- the de-generalisation specialisations reduce allocation and are enabled by the topology-invariance a general solver cannot assume, while the measurable performance comes from the convergence-level changes the same freedom unlocks. The lever is matching the method to the structure of the LQN decomposition, not importing the most sophisticated technique available.

