\section{Instrumentation: the measurement apparatus}
\label{sec:contrib-instrumentation}

Throughout this section, and in our evaluation chapter, we rely on two key pieces of instrumentation to make the spine claim measurable. The first is a correctness check. For each model in our test corpus, we run \texttt{SolverLNSimple}, \texttt{SolverLN}, and \texttt{SolverLQNS}. The test is marked as passing our solver matches either solver within 5\% tolerance on all metrics. We check both mature solver outputs as they sometimes differ in results. The second is the performance counters. Iteration counts for each solver are retrieved (\texttt{iters[0]}, \texttt{SolverLN.maxitererr.size()} and \texttt{SolverLQNS.result.iter} respectively), along with a clock execution time, allowing us to compare performance across solvers. 

The iteration counters are not strictly comparable across solvers. \texttt{SolverLN}'s counter includes the hard-reset verification pass, while the simple solver's does not, therefore the asymmetry biases the comparison slightly toward the simple solver. Execution time is not strictly comparable either, as \texttt{SolverLNSimple} and \texttt{SolverLN} are running in the same JVM while \texttt{SolverLQNS} calls an external process, which is written in C\#. Despite this, they both work as practical performance comparison metrics.



\section{Closing remarks}
\label{sec:contrib-closing}

The chapter has presented \texttt{SolverLNSimple} as a set of capabilities: a minimal LQN solver and the features that extend it, a performance optimisation of the resulting correct solver (Section~\ref{sec:DRAFT-perf-optimisation}), a short instrumentation section that makes their claims measurable, and a discussion against related work and the evaluation results. Each capability responded to a specific model failure that a design without it could not handle, and the machinery it adds is what its specific failure demanded. The \texttt{LqnGraph} abstraction emerged from the graph-derived coupling work (Section~\ref{sec:contrib-step2}), accumulated through multi-class support and fan-out handling, and became the class the codebase organises around. The design choice of the project was to always pick the solution that requires the least reworking/extra machinery, to prevent the code from becoming overly complex.

The chapter has not measured what falls out. Evaluation reports the iteration counts and accuracy data on closed-LQN fixtures spanning single-class chains, multi-class shared-callee topologies, deep IS topologies at large populations, fan-out callers with sibling callees and the multi-class-multi-entry interactions. We compare against \texttt{SolverLN} \cite{casale2024} and LQNS V5 \cite{franks2009,franks2022manual}. The structural claim the chapter has made, that the simple solver omits adaptive under-relaxation, moving-average convergence with hard-reset verification, and the unconditional \texttt{ptaskcallers} random walk, and that those omissions are not failures but designs, is what makes the iteration-count comparison meaningful when Evaluation reports it. The contribution is the construction itself, with its characterisation that the bottom-up build hits fewer iterations than both comparators, and the per-machinery decomposition in Evaluation showing that most of the iteration-count difference with \texttt{SolverLN} is structural rather than algorithmic, but not universally unnecessary.

